---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { href } from '../lib/site'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — UN/TDED"
  description="What UN/TDED is, who operates it, and the ISO/TC 154 resolution mandate behind the open-source maintenance of ISO 7372 (UNTDED)."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-un-deep">About</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-mono text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      UN<span class="wordmark-slash">/</span>TDED
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 font-serif text-xl text-body">
      <span><strong class="text-ink">UN</strong> — United Nations</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="font-mono text-un">/</span>
      <span><strong class="text-ink">TDED</strong> — Trade Data Elements Directory</span>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body">One slash, the whole standards family. Keep reading.</p>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Mission</h2>
      <p class="prose-def mt-3">
        The Trade Data Elements Directory is the lexicon of trade: the controlled
        vocabulary that defines every data element on an aligned trade document —
        its number, name, definition and representation. This project publishes
        the 2005 edition (ECE/TRADE/362, ISO 7372:2005) as a machine-readable,
        openly verifiable dataset, so that the vocabulary of trade can be
        maintained and consumed as data, not as a scanned PDF.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">The wordmark</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:items-center">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 rounded-md border border-line bg-mist p-5">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center border-l-4 border-un bg-white px-4 py-2 font-mono text-3xl tabular-nums text-ink">1001</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">UN<span class="wordmark-slash">/</span>TDED</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
          <li>
            <span class="inline-block h-3 w-3 rounded-[2px] bg-un align-middle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink">UN blue #009EDB</strong> — the official blue of the United
            Nations (Pantone 2925), used for the slash, the accents and the tag-plate edge.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="inline-block h-3 w-3 rounded-[2px] bg-flag align-middle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="inline-block h-3 w-3 rounded-[2px] bg-cat-5 align-middle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink">The category ramp</strong> — an ordered blue→teal→slate
            scale for the nine tag ranges, because tag ranges are ordered.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-ink">The slash</strong> — the UN standards-family notation:
            UN/CEFACT, UN/EDIFACT, UN/TDED. The directory belongs to that family,
            and the wordmark writes its name the family way.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-ink">The tag plate</strong> — a bordered plate carrying a
            four-digit tag, echoing the pre-printed field boxes of aligned trade
            documents. The tag is the directory’s lookup key.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-body">
        The identity is UN-like by palette and register; it does not use the UN
        emblem or name as a logo, which the United Nations reserves to itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Operation and governance</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-body">
        This site and its repositories are maintained and operated{' '}
        <strong class="text-ink">on behalf of the United Nations Economic Commission
        for Europe (UNECE) — UN/CEFACT</strong>, and the{' '}
        <strong class="text-ink">International Organization for Standardization,
        Technical Committee 154</strong> (ISO/TC 154, Processes, data elements and
        documents in commerce, industry and administration), which jointly
        maintain ISO 7372 / the TDED through their joint working group
        ISO/TC 154/JWG 9. Hosting and code are provided by the{' '}
        <a class="text-un-deep underline decoration-un/50" href="https://github.com/untded">untded</a>
        {' '}open-source organization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 rounded-md border border-line bg-mist p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">The mandate</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body">
        The work is mandated by <strong class="text-ink">ISO/TC 154 document
        N1727</strong> — resolutions of the 45th plenary meeting of ISO/TC 154,
        DIN Berlin, 2026-08-31 and 2026-09-04 (all resolutions approved by the
        six participating members DIN, SAC, BSI, JISC, UNI, KATS):
      </p>
      <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-4 border-un bg-white p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink">
        <p class="font-semibold">Resolution P-2026-07 (JWG 9) — Encouragement for exploring the use of an
        open-source platform to support the maintenance and publication of ISO 7372</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-body">
          “ISO/TC154 resolve to explore the use of an open-source platform (such
          as the pilot UN Gitlab environment) to support the maintenance and
          publication of ISO 7372. ISO/TC 154 also request that ISO Central
          Secretariat can share best practices on OSD and Smart Standards
          developed that could support this work.”
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
        Companion resolutions: <strong class="text-ink">P-2026-06</strong> (JWG 9)
        calls for strengthened ISO- and UNECE-side expert support for ISO 7372’s
        “timely publication before October 2028”;{' '}
        <strong class="text-ink">P-2026-01</strong>
        {' '}(JWG 1) initiates a meeting with the UNECE Secretariat on the stalled
        maintenance and publication of the UN/EDIFACT Directories (D.25A
        severely delayed). This platform — dataset, verification tooling and
        website — is the open-source platform those resolutions call for, with
        UNTDED 2005 as its first complete, verifiable edition.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Licensing and attribution</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
        The directory content is the UN publication ECE/TRADE/362 (in parallel
        as ISO 7372:2005), © United Nations / UNECE, reproduced with
        attribution. The digitization, its code and this website are open
        source under MIT. See{' '}
        <a class="text-un-deep underline decoration-un/50" href={href('/download')}>download</a>
        {' '}for the exact artifacts and the{' '}
        <a class="text-un-deep underline decoration-un/50" href={href('/method')}>method</a>
        {' '}page for how every character was verified against the source.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>